# Bounds agent-instructions template — Cursor (.cursorrules).
#
# Copy this file to your project root as .cursorrules, or adapt it into a project rule at
# .cursor/rules/bounds.mdc (set "alwaysApply: true" in that file's frontmatter). Cursor
# loads these rules into every request, so keep the guidance short and action-oriented.

## Architecture: Bounds

This project uses Bounds (https://bounds.dev) for architecture modeling — subsystem
boundary contracts validated against source with tree-sitter (zero LLM). Manifests live in
the hidden .bounds/ directory (.bounds/manifests/<name>.yaml). Do not open them directly;
use the `bounds` CLI, which emits JSON by default.

Before significant edits:
- Run `bounds list` to see the subsystems and how they connect.
- Run `bounds describe <subsystem>` for the public surface (exposes), dependencies
  (consumes), and dependents (consumed_by) of the subsystem you are about to change.
  Read this instead of raw source to understand a boundary.
- Run `bounds describe --namespace <ns>` to describe a whole domain group at once.
- Run `bounds overview` for file counts, language breakdown, and validation status.

After changing exports or cross-subsystem imports:
- Run `bounds validate --quick` (sub-200ms). If validation_status is not "fresh", update
  the affected manifest so the contract matches the source again.
- Run `bounds preflight` before opening a PR for boundary, contract, cycle, and orphan
  checks.

When validation_status is "fresh", trust the manifest and skip reading source. When it is
"stale" or "unresolved", run `bounds validate` first, then trust.
